WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.320
GOOGLE STADIA:
Over the years, Google has had some spectacular&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.320 --> 00:00:10.000
failures from Google Glass and Google+ to Google&nbsp;
VR and the Google Nexus. But, while unfortunate,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:15.920
these failures are what made Google. It was always&nbsp;
their willingness to experiment and shoot for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.920 --> 00:00:21.800
moon that created so many successes along the&nbsp;
way whether that be Chrome, Android, YouTube, or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.800 --> 00:00:28.080
Gmail. But, one failure that was unlike the rest&nbsp;
was actually one of their most recent failures:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.080 --> 00:00:33.080
Google Stadia. If you’re not familiar with Stadia,&nbsp;
it was Google’s attempt at entering the gaming&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.080 --> 00:00:38.560
space and more specifically the cloud gaming&nbsp;
space. The idea was that instead of spending&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.560 --> 00:00:43.720
hundreds of dollars on a console or thousands of&nbsp;
dollars on a PC, you could just stream 4k games&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.720 --> 00:00:49.400
directly from Google. You simply pay an upfront&nbsp;
fee of $130 for the starter kit and then a $10&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.400 --> 00:00:54.160
per month subscription afterwards. From the&nbsp;
very beginning, Stadia didn’t quite live up to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.160 --> 00:00:59.920
Google’s expectations. For example, in 2020, they&nbsp;
were targeting 1 million monthly active users,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.520 --> 00:01:06.400
but they ended up falling a short at 750,000. This&nbsp;
was nothing to scoff at though as it gave them a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.400 --> 00:01:12.240
5-10% share of the entire cloud gaming market&nbsp;
within just 2 years of launch. We should also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.240 --> 00:01:17.200
mention that Microsoft and Sony’s cloud gaming&nbsp;
marketshares are largely skewed to the upside&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.200 --> 00:01:22.280
because their offerings come along with Game Pass&nbsp;
Ultimate and PlayStation Plus Premium. As such,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.280 --> 00:01:26.840
a lot of these subscribers did not even use&nbsp;
Microsoft and Sony’s cloud gaming offerings&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.840 --> 00:01:32.160
but they’re nonetheless counted for market share&nbsp;
purposes. So, within the niche of cloud gaming,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.160 --> 00:01:36.480
Stadia was actually doing really well.&nbsp;
It garnered some rather strong reviews&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.480 --> 00:01:41.520
despite being a subscription model and being from&nbsp;
Google, two things that people are usually not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.520 --> 00:01:46.880
the biggest fans of. And while it obviously&nbsp;
had it’s issues like any other new product,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.880 --> 00:01:51.760
gamers were rather optimistic about the upcoming&nbsp;
Stadia exclusives and the cloud gaming space in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.760 --> 00:01:57.520
general. But for whatever reason, on September&nbsp;
29, 2022, Google announced that they would be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.520 --> 00:02:03.120
shutting down Stadia at the start of 2023.&nbsp;
This was a direction that even Google’s own&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.120 --> 00:02:08.440
internal Stadia team wasn’t aware of. In fact,&nbsp;
the Stadia team was publicly assuring users that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.440 --> 00:02:13.960
Stadia wasn’t going anywhere just 2 months before&nbsp;
this. As such, shutting down Stadia was very much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.960 --> 00:02:19.280
an executive decision that was made as part of a&nbsp;
broad effort to cut costs across the company. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.280 --> 00:02:29.760
here’s the story of the time that Google&nbsp;
idiotically killed a promising product.

00:02:29.760 --> 00:02:32.600
PROMISING BEGINNINGS:
Taking a look back,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.600 --> 00:02:38.520
the story of Stadia takes us back to the beginning&nbsp;
of 2018 to a man named Phil Harrison. Phil was a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.520 --> 00:02:44.120
lifelong gaming executive having entered the&nbsp;
industry with his own game studio back in 1986.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.120 --> 00:02:49.040
Since then, he had risen to be the president&nbsp;
of Sony’s worldwide playstation studios and a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.040 --> 00:02:55.000
VP on Microsoft’s Xbox team. He also held board&nbsp;
member seats at several gaming companies. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.000 --> 00:03:00.000
when Google started of 2018 by hiring Phil as&nbsp;
VP, it was clear that Google was entering the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:04.560
gaming space. They would spend the rest of 2018&nbsp;
attending a bunch of gaming events to recruit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.560 --> 00:03:09.360
game developers who could make their ambitious&nbsp;
visiona reality. You see, Google wasn’t just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.360 --> 00:03:14.400
trying to create a game streaming service but a&nbsp;
game streaming service that could be run on Chrome&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.400 --> 00:03:19.600
itself. And somehow, Google’s new engineers&nbsp;
would make this wizardry viable by the end of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.600 --> 00:03:25.520
2018 itself when they quietly launched a limited&nbsp;
beta of Assassin’s Creed Odyssey. A couple months&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.520 --> 00:03:31.240
later in March of 2019, Google would host a more&nbsp;
formal unveiling of Stadia, and later that year,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.240 --> 00:03:37.400
Google would officially launch Stadia on November&nbsp;
18, 2019 with a total of 22 launch titles. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.400 --> 00:03:42.720
consensus of the launch was pretty ubiquitous.&nbsp;
Almost all reviewers agreed that the technology&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.720 --> 00:03:48.080
itself was nothing short of revolutionary. With&nbsp;
the right internet connection, Stadia truly did&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.080 --> 00:03:54.040
deliver a revolutionary gaming experience,&nbsp;
but that was also their biggest critic, you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.040 --> 00:03:59.280
needed the right internet connection preferably&nbsp;
fiber which most people simply did not have. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.280 --> 00:04:03.400
while the tech behind the Stadia was heavily&nbsp;
praised, reviewers were skeptical regarding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.400 --> 00:04:08.560
whether that tech was actually applicable to the&nbsp;
average person. But, this was nothing unique to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.560 --> 00:04:14.200
Stadia. This was the case with every cloud gaming&nbsp;
solution, most of which Stadia was far ahead of in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.200 --> 00:04:19.440
every other aspect. Aside from some concerns from&nbsp;
reviewers, there were also some diehard gamers who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.440 --> 00:04:24.800
weren’t the biggest fan of Stadia. Usually though,&nbsp;
these were just enthusiasts with unrealistic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.800 --> 00:04:29.720
expectations for a brand new cloud streaming&nbsp;
service like this person who took to Reddit rant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.720 --> 00:04:34.840
about how much Stadia sucks. They didn’t&nbsp;
find much support on reddit though. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.840 --> 00:04:40.080
almost all of the replies to this thread were&nbsp;
in support of Stadia. “I play almost exclusively&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.080 --> 00:04:45.920
on Chrome. Maybe I'm just not as fussy as most&nbsp;
people.” “200 hours in Borderlands 3 on Chrome,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.920 --> 00:04:52.080
I had no idea it sucked the whole time.” “No it&nbsp;
doesn't suck. No issues here whatsoever. Maybe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.080 --> 00:04:57.760
your setup/internet/isp just sucks.” “I think&nbsp;
this is more of a you problem and not a Chrome&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.760 --> 00:05:03.280
problem. Everything I've played looks great in&nbsp;
Chrome.” “I have no issues on chrome.” “Weird,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.280 --> 00:05:08.200
I've been playing on chrome and it's fantastic.”&nbsp;
This isn’t to say that the OP didn’t have valid&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.200 --> 00:05:13.680
concerns. It’s basically a certainty that Stadia&nbsp;
was inferior to dedicated gaming rigs worth $1000s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.680 --> 00:05:19.200
of dollars. This, however, wasn’t the target&nbsp;
market for Stadia. The target market was casual&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.200 --> 00:05:24.080
gamers who were looking for a cheap way to enjoy&nbsp;
decent gaming, and that’s exactly what Stadia&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.080 --> 00:05:29.240
offered and a lot of people loved it. That’s why&nbsp;
Stadia was able to reach 750,000 monthly active&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.240 --> 00:05:36.240
users by the end of 2020 itself. For any new game&nbsp;
company, these would’ve been insane numbers, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.240 --> 00:05:49.024
for Google, they were rather disappointing&nbsp;
leading us into Google’s main dilemma with Stadia.

00:05:49.024 --> 00:05:49.680
JUST NOT ENOUGH:
If you look at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.680 --> 00:05:55.240
any of the public statements Google has made about&nbsp;
Stadia, you’ll immediately see one common thread:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.240 --> 00:06:01.000
just not enough. It wasn’t that Stadia wasn’t&nbsp;
profitable or that the tech didn’t work or that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.000 --> 00:06:05.720
their core users didn’t like it. Rather it was&nbsp;
simply that everything that Stadia had achieved&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.720 --> 00:06:11.600
was simply not enough. Honestly, this is extremely&nbsp;
confusing from Google because historically,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.600 --> 00:06:16.080
they’ve had to wait several years if not over&nbsp;
a decade before many of their products became&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.080 --> 00:06:21.720
lucrative, and a lot of those products cost&nbsp;
a lot more maintain like YouTube. I mean,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.720 --> 00:06:26.480
it’s no surprise that storing billions of hours&nbsp;
of videos for free is extremely expensive. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.480 --> 00:06:31.720
that’s why it took Google 10 to 15 years before&nbsp;
YouTube became a profitable venture. And what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.720 --> 00:06:36.200
about the Pixel? Google has been trying to break&nbsp;
into the smartphone market since the beginning of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.200 --> 00:06:42.920
smartphones, yet they still don’t even control 1%&nbsp;
of the global smartphone market. Not to mention,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.920 --> 00:06:47.520
Pixels aren’t sold with very big margins, so&nbsp;
Google may very well be losing money with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.520 --> 00:06:53.680
Pixel, but they refuse to give in. It’s the same&nbsp;
thing with Google Fiber and Google+ and Google&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.680 --> 00:06:58.560
Fi and Google Meets and Google’s enterprise cloud&nbsp;
business and several other Google services. While&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.560 --> 00:07:03.680
these never quite lived up to their expectations,&nbsp;
Google has given them plenty of time and immense&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.680 --> 00:07:09.320
resources to succeed. And it was only when there&nbsp;
was absolutely no way forward that they finally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.320 --> 00:07:14.720
threw in the towel. The same, however, cannot be&nbsp;
said about Stadia were they almost immediately&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.720 --> 00:07:19.720
threw in the towel when they saw underwhelming&nbsp;
performance. And one of the main reasons for this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.720 --> 00:07:25.320
was that they came in with extremely unrealistic&nbsp;
expectations. To be honest, I don’t even think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.320 --> 00:07:29.520
they were aiming to overtake other streaming&nbsp;
services like GeForce NOW which has 20 million&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.520 --> 00:07:35.760
registered users. Just take look at how Sundar&nbsp;
described Stadia. “With Google, your games will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.760 --> 00:07:43.880
be immediately discoverable by 2 billion people&nbsp;
on a Chrome browser, Chromebook, Chromecast, Pixel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.880 --> 00:08:19.920
device. And we have plans to support more browsers&nbsp;
and platforms over time.” Hearing that, it seems&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:19.920 --> 00:08:24.200
that Google was hoping to create something on the&nbsp;
scale of the Play Store or Android straight off&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.200 --> 00:08:29.960
the bat just because Stadia was accessible by so&nbsp;
many people. You don’t have to be a genius to see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.960 --> 00:08:35.080
how that’s a rather faulty line of reasoning. Just&nbsp;
because Google could put in front of billions of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:35.080 --> 00:08:40.160
people doesn’t mean that billions of people would&nbsp;
be interested. In reality, the cloud gaming market&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.160 --> 00:08:46.920
is still very much a new industry that was only&nbsp;
worth $1.25 billion in total in 2021. So, Stadia’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:46.920 --> 00:08:53.720
estimated revenue of $76 million accounted for&nbsp;
over 5% of this within just 2 years of launch.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:53.720 --> 00:08:58.800
Even if Stadia didn’t grow their marketshare and&nbsp;
simply grew with market, their 5% share may have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.800 --> 00:09:05.400
been worth $2 billion worth of revenue by 2030. If&nbsp;
they were able to grow to 20 or 25%, it may have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:05.400 --> 00:09:10.480
been worth closer to $10 billion worth of revenue&nbsp;
resulting in a business worth tens of billions.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.480 --> 00:09:16.480
For perspective, that by itself would be a Fortune&nbsp;
500 company. But, Google wasn’t willing to stick&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.480 --> 00:09:29.120
it out which brings me into their next pitfall&nbsp;
which is that they couldn’t handle being early.

00:09:29.120 --> 00:09:31.320
BEING EARLY IS HARD:
People will often tell you that you’ve&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:31.320 --> 00:09:37.000
wanna be early to a market. That’s how you get the&nbsp;
first mover advantage and dominate your industry,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:37.000 --> 00:09:42.200
but ironically, Google has never done&nbsp;
that ever. All of their most successful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:42.200 --> 00:09:47.160
products were either launched in line with&nbsp;
market trends or extremely late. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:47.160 --> 00:09:52.440
Google was years if not decades late to&nbsp;
search, email, and browsers. As such,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:52.440 --> 00:09:57.240
every industry they disrupted already had&nbsp;
a massive audience. Even in the industries&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:57.240 --> 00:10:00.840
that they Google failed like social&nbsp;
media and video conferencing, they’ve&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:00.840 --> 00:10:05.920
really never had a user issue, they’ve only&nbsp;
had retention issues. With Google+ for example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:05.920 --> 00:10:12.920
Google boasted 540 million users. Now, 90% of&nbsp;
sessions didn’t even last 5 seconds but again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:12.920 --> 00:10:18.720
that’s a retention issue, not a user issue. In&nbsp;
basically every industry that Google has entered,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:18.720 --> 00:10:23.200
getting users was never really a problem for&nbsp;
them because of their ubiquitous nature. All&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:23.200 --> 00:10:27.920
Google had to do was convince these people&nbsp;
that their solution was superior. Sometimes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:27.920 --> 00:10:32.800
they succeeded in this, sometimes they didn’t,&nbsp;
and they approached Stadia with this exact same&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:32.800 --> 00:10:39.280
strategy. They created a world class solution and&nbsp;
put it in front of people, but there was one major&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:39.280 --> 00:10:44.480
difference. The cloud gaming market was yet to&nbsp;
truly develop, meaning that there was no existing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:44.480 --> 00:10:50.840
audience to convince. Rather, it was up to Google&nbsp;
to appeal to new users and create that market. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:50.840 --> 00:10:55.720
it seems that this was not a journey that Google&nbsp;
was especially excited to venture down because the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:55.720 --> 00:11:01.640
reality is that being early is usually way harder&nbsp;
than being late, so they decided to pull the plug&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:01.640 --> 00:11:06.440
in an extremely expensive manner. They promised&nbsp;
to refund all Stadia hardware purchases made&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:06.440 --> 00:11:10.880
through the Google Store, and all game and add-on&nbsp;
content purchased through the Stadia store. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:10.880 --> 00:11:17.160
resulted in refunds of up to $900 per person.&nbsp;
Instead of spending all that money on refunds,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:17.160 --> 00:11:22.840
Google could have just kept Stadia alive and seen&nbsp;
how far it took them. After all, it’s not like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:22.840 --> 00:11:27.840
the operating expenses of Stadia were really&nbsp;
a concern for Google. Also, it’s not like the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:27.840 --> 00:11:32.680
cloud gaming market isn’t suddenly gonna stop&nbsp;
growing without Google. It’ll continue to grow&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:32.680 --> 00:11:38.160
into a substantial market and it’ll probabaly just&nbsp;
be controlled Microsoft, Sony, Nvidia, and maybe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:38.160 --> 00:11:44.240
Nintendo. In the grand scheme of things, I guess&nbsp;
it doesn’t matter because Google is so big at this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:44.240 --> 00:11:50.080
point that even another $20 billion in revenue&nbsp;
from Stadia doesn’t even make a difference,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:50.080 --> 00:11:55.800
but that doesn’t change that what happened to&nbsp;
Stadia was still quite a shame. And that is the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:55.800 --> 00:12:00.440
story of the time that Google killed a promising&nbsp;
product simply due to unrealistic expectations&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:00.440 --> 00:12:06.200
and a lack of patience and commitment. Unlike&nbsp;
Google, someone who adamant on not giving up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:06.200 --> 00:12:11.400
on the gaming market despite terrible economics&nbsp;
is Microsoft who just dived in even deeper with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:11.400 --> 00:12:16.440
their $70 billion acquisition of Activision&nbsp;
Blizzard. Check out this video to learn more.

